          "id": "practice-beginner",
          "kind": "choice",
          "units": 1,
          "prompt": "X is 0 or 2, each with probability 1/2. What is its variance?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "signed",
              "text": "0",
              "feedback": "Signed deviations cancel, but variance averages squared deviations."
            },
            {
              "id": "variance",
              "text": "1",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "id": "moment",
              "text": "2",
              "feedback": "That is the second raw moment. Subtract the squared mean."
            },
            {
              "id": "range",
              "text": "4",
              "feedback": "The squared range is not the mean squared distance from the mean."
            }
          ],
          "on_correct": "practice-intermediate",
          "on_wrong": "practice-intermediate",
          "tests": "variance calculation",
          "solution": "The mean is 1. Both squared deviations are 1, so their equally weighted average is 1."
        },
        {
          "id": "practice-intermediate",
          "kind": "numeric",
          "units": 1,
          "prompt": "X takes 1 with probability 1/4 and 3 with probability 3/4. Y=2X-7. Compute Var(Y), dimensionless.",
          "answer": {
            "value": 3,
            "tolerance": 1e-08
          },
          "feedback_wrong": "Use the nonuniform probabilities to find Var(X), then scale the variance by 2 squared. The shift changes no variance.",
          "on_correct": "practice-advanced",
          "on_wrong": "practice-advanced",
          "tests": "nonuniform finite variance and rescaling",
          "solution": "E[X]=5/2 and E[X^2]=7, so Var(X)=7-25/4=3/4. Hence Var(Y)=4 times 3/4=3; the additive constant has no effect."
        },
        {
          "id": "practice-advanced",
          "kind": "short",
          "units": 1,
          "prompt": "Novel synthetic sensor-fusion transfer: centered errors U,V have variances 4 and 9 mV² and covariance 1 mV². A device averages them as W=(U+V)/2. Derive Var(W), include its unit, and explain what changes if zero covariance is justified. Do these second moments alone prove independence?",
          "criteria": [
            "Expand the centered square or use covariance bilinearity to obtain (Var(U)+Var(V)+2Cov(U,V))/4.",
            "Compute 15/4 mV², and 13/4 mV² if covariance is zero.",
            "State finite second moments and distinguish zero covariance from independence; independence is sufficient but not established by these moments."
          ],
          "next": "end",
          "tests": "transfer variance to correlated sensor averaging",
          "solution": "Finite second moments permit the covariance expansion. Var(W)=(Var(U)+Var(V)+2Cov(U,V))/4=(4+9+2)/4=15/4 mV². With zero covariance it becomes 13/4 mV². Independence would imply zero covariance here, but zero covariance and the listed moments do not establish independence."
        }
      ]
    },
